NoteThe Law of the Wave



The graph of sin is the height of the point after walking x. It repeats every 2π, the length of one walk round.

Four dials
Ride a Ferris wheel. Your height, as the wheel turns, is a sine wave: the circle of part 1, stood on its edge. Change the wheel, and the wave changes with it. There are only a few things to change.








The wheel has radius 3 instead of 1. How far above the hub do you go?
3, and 3 below it. Every height is 3 times as big: y = 3 sin x.


What is that 3 called?
The amplitude: how far the wave goes above and below its middle.


The hub is raised 1. What happens to every height?
It goes up 1: y = sin x + 1.


Where is the middle of the wave now?
At y = 1, the hub’s height. That line is the midline.


The wheel turns twice as fast. How long does one lap take?
Half as long: π instead of 2π. y = sin 2x repeats every π.


What is the length of one lap called?
The period.


Why does a bigger number mean a shorter period?
Turning B times as fast fits B laps into 2π, so one lap takes 2π/B.


You get on 1 later. What happens to the wave?
Everything happens 1 later: the wave slides 1 to the right. y = sin(x − 1).


Why minus, if it moves right?
You start your ride when x − 1 = 0, that is, at x = 1. The start has moved from 0 to 1.



Now turn the dials yourself. The grey wave is y = sin x, for comparison.

